diff --git a/docs/development.md b/docs/development.md
index a064bdc..0b6d287 100644
--- a/docs/development.md
+++ b/docs/development.md
@@ -510,7 +510,7 @@ A component whose source is a text key - the title, the subtitle and the text of
 
 `addComponent()` and `addStack()` register one: they call `\Nino\Html::addShortcode()` with a wrapper around the callback, and **replace** a component or a shortcode of the same name, which is how a project changes the markup of one. The rest of the API - `components()` and `stacks()`, `defaults()`, `sources()`, `value()`, `element()`, `renderStack()`, `cell()` and `escape()` - is documented where it stands, in `Components.php`. `sources()` is what a tool reads a template with: the keys and the slots it names as the source of a component, found as the rendering finds the calls - and the slots it names in an attribute of the type `image`, a card's `background` among them, as an entry that says which attribute it was. The Text Keys scan reads them besides the fills, the Image Slots scan besides the `<img>` tags, and both lists of where a key or a slot is used read them, since a page the Builder writes names its texts and pictures in no other way.
 
-**The schema.** `label` is a string or a locale map, and every attribute needs a `default`. An attribute is a `string`, an `int` (`min`, `max`), a `bool`, a `select` (`options`, a list, with the default among them) or `lines`, and four types that only exist here: `key` (a text key), `image` (an image slot), `href` (a key or an address) and `variant`. A **variant** is a `select` whose words are the modifiers of one class: the renderer writes a word that is not empty as that class, the `prefix` and the word - the prefix is the start of a class that ends in two hyphens (`nino-ink--`) where the schema says it, and the class the component is named for with two hyphens where it does not, so that `style="loud"` of a title is `nino-section-title--loud` - and so a variant's options are words, lower case letters and digits with hyphens between them, never a class name: that is how a tool tells a variant from a choice of something else, and draws it as a row of buttons by default. `label`, `hint` and `input` of an attribute are optional. `input` is a hint for a tool on how to draw the attribute, and nothing a renderer reads: for a `select` and a `variant` `select` (the default), `segment` (the words as a row of buttons) or `icons` (the icons of an alignment - the options are the empty word and words of `left`, `center` and `right`, of `top`, `middle`, `bottom` and `last-bottom` (a card's own) or of `start`, `center` and `end`), for a `bool` `switch` (the default) or `checkbox`; no other type has one. `class` is allowed on every component without being declared: the renderer puts it at the end of the classes of its outer element. `draws`, `in`, `holds`, `icon`, `link` and `by` are optional and tell a tool something about a component without changing how it renders - but for `link`, which does. `draws` is what the renderer draws on the outside, written the way a builder shows it: the tag, a space, the one class a theme addresses the element by - `{tag} .nino-section-title`, `a .nino-btn`. It is a non-empty string of at most 64 characters from `a-z`, `0-9`, space, `.`, `{`, `}` and `-`, and a name in braces is an attribute of the schema, put in from the shortcode or else from the default. `in` says where a tool offers the component, and a place says where a component stands: a non-empty list of distinct words from `section` (directly in a column - also in the cell of a stack that holds no card), `card` (in a card) and `hero` (directly in a column of the hero section, the first section of a page when it has a cover); without it the component is offered everywhere. `holds` is one of the same three words, and only a component whose source is its `content` may have it: what stands between its tags is then the calls of the components of that place, which are rendered with it and which a builder edits as its children. The kernel has one, `[card]`, which holds `card`. `icon` is the picture a tool shows the component by - a tool shows one of its own where there is none - and it is geometry and not markup: a list of 1 to 12 parts of a 24 by 24 icon that the tool draws with a stroke, each part a tag (`path`, `circle`, `ellipse`, `rect`, `line`, `polyline` or `polygon`) and the map of the attributes that tag takes, every value a string, as in `[ 'path', [ 'd' => 'M6 12h12' ] ]`; the kernel's fifteen carry the icons of Lucide. `link` is a bool, false where the schema says nothing: a component that says `true` has two more attributes, `href` (a key, an address or in a stack a field of the element, as a button's) and `target` (`_blank` or none), after the ones it declares and before `class`, and where `href` comes out as an address what its renderer drew is wrapped in `<a href="...">`, with `target="_blank" rel="noopener"` for `_blank` - the address is made safe as a button's is, a scheme that is none a link may take is a `#`. It stands for a component that draws one thing and no link of its own: the schema may not declare `href` or `target` itself, and a text that holds a link of its own would be a link inside a link. The `<a>` is the outer element of a linked component, so a rule that names the component as the direct child of something (`.nino-card-cols > .nino-card-img`, `.nino-text-center > .nino-section-text--loud`) does not match it while it is linked: write the rule for the anchor as well, or without the `>`. `by` names who registered a component - a slug, which a tool groups what it offers by: `nino` where the schema says nothing, which is the kernel's own components and what a module of a project registers, and the key of the feature for the components of a feature's manifest, whatever the manifest says of it. `group` is the word a tool offers a component under - `section`, `hero`, `card` or `global` - and what `by` says decides whether it has one: a component of the kernel or of a module of a project (`by` is `nino`) has to declare it, as one of the four, and one of a feature's manifest declares none, since a tool offers it under the feature: a manifest that names one is refused. It is not `in`: a place says where a component may stand and a group where it is offered, so that the title of a hero, which stands in a section as well (`in` is `[ 'hero', 'section' ]`), is offered with the hero (`hero`) - and `global` is the group of what stands anywhere, a text, a picture, a button, the html and a spacer. A component that draws another element in another place is another component - the kernel has six titles - because no renderer reads a context. A stack declares none of the six, nor a `group`. A stack says `grid => true` if it takes `cols`, `gap` and `autoheight` and draws cells, and may name `assets` (`css` and `js`, project paths) for a tool to tell the developer which files it needs - nothing adds them to a page. `preview` names one of the fixed pictures a builder draws it as. The names `value`, `source`, `content`, `text`, `uri` and `grid` are taken, and so are the arguments of a stack's loop. **A schema the module cannot work with is refused at registration** with an `E_USER_ERROR` that says why: a name that is no slug, an unknown `source` or type, a `select` with no options or a default outside them, an attribute without a default, an `int` outside its bounds, a place in `in` that is none of the three or is named twice, a `holds` that names no place or stands with another source than `content`, a `draws` that is empty, of other characters or more than 64 or with a placeholder that no attribute answers, an `icon` that is no such list of parts or holds more than 2048 characters of values, a variant with an option that is no word or a prefix that is no start of a class, an `input` that its type does not have or icons for words that are no alignment, a `link` that is no bool or that declares an `href` or a `target` of its own, a `by` that is no slug, a `group` that a component of the kernel or of a project lacks or that is none of the four, a `group` in a component of a feature. A manifest is read the same way, before it is applied (see [Developing a feature](features.md#components-and-stacks)). Rendering does not validate: an attribute that is not declared is ignored, a `select` outside its options is the default, a number that is none too, so a file written by hand may be incomplete and still renders.
+**The schema.** `label` is a string or a locale map, and every attribute needs a `default`. An attribute is a `string`, an `int` (`min`, `max`), a `bool`, a `select` (`options`, a list, with the default among them) or `lines`, and four types that only exist here: `key` (a text key), `image` (an image slot), `href` (a key or an address) and `variant`. A **variant** is a `select` whose words are the modifiers of one class: the renderer writes a word that is not empty as that class, the `prefix` and the word - the prefix is the start of a class that ends in two hyphens (`nino-ink--`) where the schema says it, and the class the component is named for with two hyphens where it does not, so that `style="loud"` of a title is `nino-section-title--loud` - and so a variant's options are words, lower case letters and digits with hyphens between them, never a class name: that is how a tool tells a variant from a choice of something else, and draws it as a row of buttons by default. `label`, `hint`, `input` and `tab` of an attribute are optional. `input` is a hint for a tool on how to draw the attribute, and nothing a renderer reads: for a `select` and a `variant` `select` (the default), `segment` (the words as a row of buttons) or `icons` (the icons of an alignment - the options are the empty word and words of `left`, `center` and `right`, of `top`, `middle`, `bottom` and `last-bottom` (a card's own) or of `start`, `center` and `end`), for a `bool` `switch` (the default) or `checkbox`; no other type has one. `tab` is the other hint of that kind, on an attribute of any type: one word of the short list the registry knows (`\Nino\Modules\Components::TABS`, today `background`), which says that a tool draws the attribute on the tab of that name in its dialog for the component - the Builder draws the attributes of a tab together on a tab of their own, after the properties - and so knows no component by name; an attribute that says none stands among the properties, and has no such key in the registry. The kernel's card says `background` for its `ground`, `background`, `focus` and `dim`. `class` is allowed on every component without being declared: the renderer puts it at the end of the classes of its outer element. `draws`, `in`, `holds`, `icon`, `link` and `by` are optional and tell a tool something about a component without changing how it renders - but for `link`, which does. `draws` is what the renderer draws on the outside, written the way a builder shows it: the tag, a space, the one class a theme addresses the element by - `{tag} .nino-section-title`, `a .nino-btn`. It is a non-empty string of at most 64 characters from `a-z`, `0-9`, space, `.`, `{`, `}` and `-`, and a name in braces is an attribute of the schema, put in from the shortcode or else from the default. `in` says where a tool offers the component, and a place says where a component stands: a non-empty list of distinct words from `section` (directly in a column - also in the cell of a stack that holds no card), `card` (in a card) and `hero` (directly in a column of the hero section, the first section of a page when it has a cover); without it the component is offered everywhere. `holds` is one of the same three words, and only a component whose source is its `content` may have it: what stands between its tags is then the calls of the components of that place, which are rendered with it and which a builder edits as its children. The kernel has one, `[card]`, which holds `card`. `icon` is the picture a tool shows the component by - a tool shows one of its own where there is none - and it is geometry and not markup: a list of 1 to 12 parts of a 24 by 24 icon that the tool draws with a stroke, each part a tag (`path`, `circle`, `ellipse`, `rect`, `line`, `polyline` or `polygon`) and the map of the attributes that tag takes, every value a string, as in `[ 'path', [ 'd' => 'M6 12h12' ] ]`; the kernel's fifteen carry the icons of Lucide. `link` is a bool, false where the schema says nothing: a component that says `true` has two more attributes, `href` (a key, an address or in a stack a field of the element, as a button's) and `target` (`_blank` or none), after the ones it declares and before `class`, and where `href` comes out as an address what its renderer drew is wrapped in `<a href="...">`, with `target="_blank" rel="noopener"` for `_blank` - the address is made safe as a button's is, a scheme that is none a link may take is a `#`. It stands for a component that draws one thing and no link of its own: the schema may not declare `href` or `target` itself, and a text that holds a link of its own would be a link inside a link. The `<a>` is the outer element of a linked component, so a rule that names the component as the direct child of something (`.nino-card-cols > .nino-card-img`, `.nino-text-center > .nino-section-text--loud`) does not match it while it is linked: write the rule for the anchor as well, or without the `>`. `by` names who registered a component - a slug, which a tool groups what it offers by: `nino` where the schema says nothing, which is the kernel's own components and what a module of a project registers, and the key of the feature for the components of a feature's manifest, whatever the manifest says of it. `group` is the word a tool offers a component under - `section`, `hero`, `card` or `global` - and what `by` says decides whether it has one: a component of the kernel or of a module of a project (`by` is `nino`) has to declare it, as one of the four, and one of a feature's manifest declares none, since a tool offers it under the feature: a manifest that names one is refused. It is not `in`: a place says where a component may stand and a group where it is offered, so that the title of a hero, which stands in a section as well (`in` is `[ 'hero', 'section' ]`), is offered with the hero (`hero`) - and `global` is the group of what stands anywhere, a text, a picture, a button, the html and a spacer. A component that draws another element in another place is another component - the kernel has six titles - because no renderer reads a context. A stack declares none of the six, nor a `group`. A stack says `grid => true` if it takes `cols`, `gap` and `autoheight` and draws cells, and may name `assets` (`css` and `js`, project paths) for a tool to tell the developer which files it needs - nothing adds them to a page. `preview` names one of the fixed pictures a builder draws it as. The names `value`, `source`, `content`, `text`, `uri` and `grid` are taken, and so are the arguments of a stack's loop. **A schema the module cannot work with is refused at registration** with an `E_USER_ERROR` that says why: a name that is no slug, an unknown `source` or type, a `select` with no options or a default outside them, an attribute without a default, an `int` outside its bounds, a place in `in` that is none of the three or is named twice, a `holds` that names no place or stands with another source than `content`, a `draws` that is empty, of other characters or more than 64 or with a placeholder that no attribute answers, an `icon` that is no such list of parts or holds more than 2048 characters of values, a variant with an option that is no word or a prefix that is no start of a class, an `input` that its type does not have or icons for words that are no alignment, a `tab` that is none of the words of the list, a `link` that is no bool or that declares an `href` or a `target` of its own, a `by` that is no slug, a `group` that a component of the kernel or of a project lacks or that is none of the four, a `group` in a component of a feature. A manifest is read the same way, before it is applied (see [Developing a feature](features.md#components-and-stacks)). Rendering does not validate: an attribute that is not declared is ignored, a `select` outside its options is the default, a number that is none too, so a file written by hand may be incomplete and still renders.
 
 **What a renderer receives.** `public static function componentTitle( array &$appData, array $args ): string`, with `$args` made ready by `dispatch()`: the first argument resolved by `value()` and made safe (`value`, and `source` as it was written), what stands between the tags (`content`), every attribute under its name with the defaults filled in and a `select` reduced to its options, and `text` where the shortcode had a fixed one. A component whose value is `null` - or empty, where it has a source - renders nothing: an empty slot is no broken picture, and a missing field no empty heading. What a renderer returns is rendered again like the output of any shortcode, which is why `value` is escaped and has every `[` written as `&#91;` (the rule is in [AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md#rendering-and-escaping-rules)); `value()` documents what the first argument can be - a text key, a field or the place in the loop in a stack, a fixed `text="..."`, a picture reference, an address.
 
@@ -518,7 +518,7 @@ The kernel brings the titles and subtitles of a section, a card and a hero, `[te
 
 The six titles share one renderer and differ in the class they draw, which is their place: `title` and `subtitle` are for a section, and offered in the hero as well (`in` is `[ 'hero', 'section' ]`, `group` is `section`), `hero-title` and `hero-subtitle` for a hero, and offered in a section as well (`[ 'hero', 'section' ]`, `hero`), `card-title` and `card-subtitle` for a card (`[ 'card' ]`, `card`), and `draws` names the tag and the class (`{tag} .nino-card-title`, `{tag} .nino-hero-subtitle`). `[card-text]` and `[card-image]` are the text and the picture of a card, offered in a card alone (`card`), and draw `div .nino-card-descr` and `img .nino-card-img`. `[button]` declares `draws` too, `a .nino-btn`, and is offered everywhere; `[text]`, `[image]`, `[html]` and `[spacer]` declare neither, and neither does a stack. These five, the ones that stand anywhere, are the group `global`. `[card]` declares all three: `in` is `[ 'section', 'hero' ]`, `draws` is `article .nino-card` and `holds` is `card`, and its group is `card`, as it is that of the title, the subtitle, the text and the picture of a card. `[scroll-hint]`, the arrow of a hero, is offered under `hero`. The registry carries the keys as the schema has them - `components()` and a feature's manifest are both read by `validate()`.
 
-`[card]` is a component that holds components: what stands between its tags is the calls of the components of a card - a title, a text, an image, a button - and it draws the `<article>` around them with its ground, its padding, whether it takes the whole height of its column or cell (`fill`), where its content stands in a card taller than it (`vertical`), how the text and the buttons are aligned, a picture behind the content, its border and a class of its own; `componentCard()` documents the attributes and the order of the classes. The same component stands once in a column - a card beside a picture - and once per element in a stack: the cards of a loop. It reads no context itself; the components in it do. The content is not rendered by the renderer, which calls no `renderHtml()` of its own: the engine renders the output of a shortcode again (`\Nino\Html::_doShortcode()`, to a depth it caps - that is what makes `[template]` work), so the components in the card are rendered when the card is, and in a stack with the element still their context, because `renderStack()` holds it around the rendering of the stack's content. A card with nothing between its tags renders nothing; a card whose components all render nothing is an empty `<article>`, as a section with nothing in it is a section. The markup of the card and of its picture is `$html['card']` and `$html['card-bg']`.
+`[card]` is a component that holds components: what stands between its tags is the calls of the components of a card - a title, a text, an image, a button - and it draws the `<article>` around them with its ground, its padding, whether it takes the whole height of its column or cell (`fill`), where its content stands in a card taller than it (`vertical`), how the text and the buttons are aligned, a picture behind the content, its border, whether the link of its title is the link of the whole card (`linked`) and a class of its own; `componentCard()` documents the attributes and the order of the classes. `linked` is the stretched link: the card is no `<a>` - it holds buttons, and a link in a link is no markup - but writes the class `nino-card--linked`, and `Nino.css` stretches the link that `[card-title]` draws around its title, when the title has an `href`, over the whole card by its `::after`, so that the keyboard has one stop, named by the title and shown as a ring on the card, the pointer has the whole area, and the buttons, links, fields, frames and players in the card are lifted over the overlay and keep targets of their own. A card whose title is no link shows nothing of it, and text in a linked card cannot be selected. The same component stands once in a column - a card beside a picture - and once per element in a stack: the cards of a loop. It reads no context itself; the components in it do. The content is not rendered by the renderer, which calls no `renderHtml()` of its own: the engine renders the output of a shortcode again (`\Nino\Html::_doShortcode()`, to a depth it caps - that is what makes `[template]` work), so the components in the card are rendered when the card is, and in a stack with the element still their context, because `renderStack()` holds it around the rendering of the stack's content. A card with nothing between its tags renders nothing; a card whose components all render nothing is an empty `<article>`, as a section with nothing in it is a section. The markup of the card and of its picture is `$html['card']` and `$html['card-bg']`.
 
 `[image]` is the shortcode `Modules\Images` had, and it behaves as it did in every form; `componentImage()` documents the alt text, the content form that a meta tag or a mail asks for the address with, and the frame that crops, which a focus, a ratio or a class of its own call for and which is cut at the radius of the image kind (see the radius of a surface at the end of this chapter). `[card-image]` is the picture of a card: the `<img>` of `.nino-card-img`, which is as wide as the card (`style="inline"`, the class `nino-card-img--inline`, is the other way round, the picture at its own size), or - for a focus or a ratio - the frame of `[image]`, which then carries `nino-card-img` so that the card's rules work on it. `[card-text]` is `[text]` in the class of the text of a card, `nino-card-descr`. `[button]` has a `width` besides its `size`, `full` for a button as wide as the box it stands in (`nino-btn--fullwidth`); where it has no address at all it is a `<span class="nino-btn">`, the same classes and no link, since an `<a>` without an `href` looks like a button and is none. `[scroll-hint]` is the arrow at the foot of a hero as a link: an `<a class="nino-hero-arrowdown">` to the section whose id `to` names, and with none a `#` marked `data-arrow-next`, which `Nino.ui.js` answers by scrolling to what follows the section the arrow stands in; the chevron is drawn by `Nino.css` in the ink the arrow stands on, and its label, for a screen reader, is a text of its own, `/template/common/navigation/next-section`. One thing the code does not say: the content of a shortcode runs up to the first closing tag, so a bare `[image]` ahead of the content form `[image /logo]...[/image]` in one template reads the text between them as its own content. Write the bare one as `[image /x][/image]`. The markup of every component is a property, `\Nino\Modules\Components::$html`, `$html['img']` among them - replace an entry, or register a component of the same name.
 
